    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d241">

                <pb facs="d241-01" n="1" xml:id="d241-01"/>

                <head>241. Telegram 3666 From the Embassy in India to the Department of State<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d241-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d241-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline><placeName>New Delhi</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1972-03-23T15:50:00+00:00">March 23, 1972, 1550Z</date>
                </dateline>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item> GOP Offer of Military Facilities in Pakistan</item>
                </list>
                <list type="references">
                    <head>REF:</head>
                    <item>A) State 49598</item>
                    <item>B) Islamabad 2213</item>
                    <item>C) Islamabad 2247</item>
                </list>
                <p>1. Apparent GOP decision to seek replay history of substantial US military supply
                    and collaboration against India strikes me as ominously irrational and, if
                    pursued, certain to prejudice prospects for durable peace in South Asia.</p>
                <p>2. I hope this Embassy will re consulted before any serious consideration is
                    given to the proposals contained reftels.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed> Keating </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d241-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d241-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Files 1970–73, DEF 15 PAK–US. Top
                    Secret; Exdis.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d241-f2" n="2" xml:id="d241-f2-content">
                <p>Ambassador Keating expressed his opposition to any increase in U.S. military
                    collaboration with Pakistan.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
